{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"g1whywhat0001","type":"group","x":-40,"y":-80,"width":2820,"height":440,"label":"Layer 1 — What & Why RAG","color":"6"},
		{"id":"g2pipeline0002","type":"group","x":-40,"y":460,"width":2820,"height":820,"label":"Layer 2 — Standard RAG Pipeline","color":"4"},
		{"id":"g3methods0003","type":"group","x":-40,"y":1400,"width":2820,"height":400,"label":"Layer 3 — Retrieval Methods","color":"5"},
		{"id":"g4infra00004","type":"group","x":-40,"y":1920,"width":2820,"height":420,"label":"Layer 4 — Key Infrastructure","color":"3"},
		{"id":"g5arch000005","type":"group","x":-40,"y":2460,"width":2820,"height":400,"label":"Layer 5 — RAG Architectures","color":"2"},
		{"id":"g6evalchal006","type":"group","x":-40,"y":2980,"width":2820,"height":400,"label":"Layer 6 — Evaluation & Challenges","color":"1"},

		{"id":"n1whatisrag01","type":"text","x":0,"y":0,"width":640,"height":280,"text":"# What is RAG\n\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation: combine a retrieval system (search over external knowledge) with a generative LLM.\n\nInstead of relying only on the model's frozen training weights, fetch relevant external text at query time and place it in the prompt so the model answers from *evidence*, not memory alone. Coined by Lewis et al. 2020 (Facebook AI)."},
		{"id":"n2hallucinat02","type":"text","x":700,"y":0,"width":640,"height":280,"text":"# Hallucination\n\nModels generate fluent but factually wrong text — confident, not honest.\n\nRAG is a grounding fix: force the answer to come from retrieved text instead of the model's parametric memory."},
		{"id":"n3contextwin03","type":"text","x":1400,"y":0,"width":640,"height":280,"text":"# Context Window\n\nFinite input length (tokens) a model can attend to. Can't paste an entire corpus into the prompt.\n\nForces retrieval to *select* the few relevant pieces instead of dumping everything."},
		{"id":"n4cutoffgap04","type":"text","x":2100,"y":0,"width":640,"height":280,"text":"# Knowledge Cutoff & Domain Gap\n\nTraining data has a fixed cutoff date and general-domain bias. A model has never seen private docs, recent events, or niche internal data.\n\nRetrieval bridges the gap without retraining or fine-tuning."},

		{"id":"n5indexpipe05","type":"text","x":0,"y":520,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Indexing Pipeline (offline)\n\nLoad & parse source documents (PDF, HTML, DB rows, code) → split into chunks → embed each chunk (or tokenize for a lexical index) → store in a vector database / inverted index.\n\nDone once up front, re-run whenever source data changes."},
		{"id":"n6querypipe06","type":"text","x":700,"y":520,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Query Pipeline (online)\n\nUser query → embed/tokenize the query (same method as indexing!) → retrieve top-k candidates → optionally rerank → assemble prompt (query + context) → LLM generates → optionally cite sources.\n\nRuns fresh for every user question."},
		{"id":"n7chunking007","type":"text","x":1400,"y":520,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Chunking\n\nSplit documents into retrievable units. Whole doc: too big, dilutes relevance. Single sentence: too small, loses context.\n\nStrategies: fixed-size, sliding window (overlap), structure/semantic-aware (headers, AST, paragraph boundaries). Size is a tunable tradeoff, not a fixed rule."},
		{"id":"n8embedvec008","type":"text","x":0,"y":900,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Embedding & Vector Store\n\nEach chunk is turned into a dense vector by an embedding model, then written to a vector store indexed for fast similarity search.\n\nAt query time the same embedding model encodes the question, and nearest-neighbor search finds semantically similar chunks."},
		{"id":"n9rerank0009","type":"text","x":700,"y":900,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Reranking\n\nA cross-encoder or LLM-based reranker re-scores the initial candidates for finer relevance ordering before they enter the prompt.\n\nInitial retrieval (bi-encoder/BM25) optimizes speed over a huge corpus; reranking optimizes accuracy over a small candidate set. Common pattern: retrieve top 50–100, rerank down to top 3–5."},
		{"id":"n10promptgen10","type":"text","x":1400,"y":900,"width":640,"height":280,"text":"# Prompt Augmentation & Generation\n\nConstruct the prompt: instructions + retrieved chunks + user question, explicitly telling the model to answer using only the provided context.\n\nTurns retrieval into in-context learning — no fine-tuning needed to add new knowledge."},

		{"id":"n11irbasics11","type":"text","x":0,"y":1460,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Information Retrieval (IR) Basics\n\n50-year-old field. Core vocabulary: query, document, corpus, relevance.\n\nRAG retrieval is classic IR applied to LLM context — same theory, new consumer."},
		{"id":"n12sparse0012","type":"text","x":700,"y":1460,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Sparse / Lexical Retrieval\n\nExact term matching, bag-of-words. TF-IDF: term frequent in doc, rare in corpus → higher weight. BM25: TF-IDF evolved, adds term-frequency saturation (k1) and document-length normalization (b).\n\nFast, explainable, no model needed — but blind to synonyms."},
		{"id":"n13dense00013","type":"text","x":1400,"y":1460,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Dense Retrieval\n\nEncode query and docs as vectors (embeddings), rank by cosine similarity / approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search (e.g. HNSW).\n\nCaptures semantic meaning — finds synonyms and paraphrases. Costs: needs a model, less explainable than lexical.\n\n**Real example:** DPR (Dense Passage Retriever) — BERT bi-encoder + Maximum Inner Product Search (MIPS), the retriever in the original RAG paper (Lewis et al. 2020)."},
		{"id":"n14hybrid0014","type":"text","x":2100,"y":1460,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Hybrid Retrieval\n\nCombine sparse + dense results, typically via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF): merge two ranked lists by summing 1/(rank+const) per item.\n\nGets exact-match strength (lexical) plus semantic robustness (dense). Common production default."},

		{"id":"n15embedmod15","type":"text","x":0,"y":1980,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Embedding Models\n\nNeural networks that map text to fixed-size vectors capturing semantic meaning (e.g. OpenAI text-embedding-3, BGE, Sentence-BERT).\n\nSimilar meaning → nearby vectors. Choice of embedding model matters as much as choice of LLM."},
		{"id":"n16vecdb00016","type":"text","x":700,"y":1980,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Vector Databases\n\nSpecialized stores for high-dimensional vectors with fast ANN search (HNSW, IVF indexes). Examples: Pinecone, Weaviate, Milvus, Qdrant, Chroma, pgvector.\n\nCore infra for dense retrieval at scale."},
		{"id":"n17invidx0017","type":"text","x":1400,"y":1980,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Inverted Index\n\n`term → posting list`. Classic data structure for exact-match lexical search (Elasticsearch, Lucene).\n\nWhy sparse search is fast: only score documents sharing a term with the query, skip the rest of the corpus."},
		{"id":"n18tokeniz0018","type":"text","x":2100,"y":1980,"width":640,"height":320,"text":"# Tokenization for Retrieval\n\nText → terms: lowercasing, splitting on non-alphanumerics, identifier splitting. Different goal from LLM tokenization (BPE/subword) — search recall vs compact model input.\n\nUse the *same* tokenizer/analyzer for indexing and queries, always."},

		{"id":"n19naive0019","type":"text","x":0,"y":2520,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Naive RAG\n\nThe baseline pattern: index once, retrieve top-k, stuff into prompt, generate.\n\nSimple, but brittle — no query understanding, no result verification, sensitive to chunking and retrieval quality."},
		{"id":"n20advanced20","type":"text","x":700,"y":2520,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Advanced RAG\n\nAdds pre-retrieval steps (query rewriting/expansion, HyDE — Hypothetical Document Embeddings) and post-retrieval steps (reranking, context compression/filtering) around the naive core.\n\nFixes precision and recall problems of the naive pipeline."},
		{"id":"n21modular021","type":"text","x":1400,"y":2520,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Modular RAG\n\nTreats RAG as swappable modules (routing, query fusion, memory, multiple retrievers) composed and reordered per use case, instead of one fixed linear pipeline.\n\nLets teams mix strategies per query type."},
		{"id":"n22agentic022","type":"text","x":2100,"y":2520,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Agentic RAG / GraphRAG\n\nAgentic RAG: an LLM agent decides whether/what/how many times to retrieve, can issue follow-up queries or call tools.\n\nGraphRAG: retrieves over a knowledge graph instead of flat chunks — better for multi-hop/relational questions."},

		{"id":"n23retmetric23","type":"text","x":0,"y":3040,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Retrieval Metrics\n\nRecall@k: is a relevant item in the top-k? Precision@k: what fraction of top-k is relevant? MRR: position of the first relevant hit. NDCG: rewards relevant items ranked higher.\n\nStandard IR metrics applied to the retrieval stage."},
		{"id":"n24ragmetric24","type":"text","x":700,"y":3040,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# RAG-Specific Metrics\n\nFaithfulness/groundedness: is the answer supported by retrieved context? Answer relevancy: does it address the question? Context precision/recall: are retrieved chunks relevant and complete?\n\nPopular framework: RAGAS."},
		{"id":"n25lostmid025","type":"text","x":1400,"y":3040,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Lost in the Middle\n\nModels attend better to the start and end of a long context, worse to the middle (U-shaped attention curve).\n\nMatters for chunk ordering in the prompt — put the most relevant chunk first or last, not buried in the middle."},
		{"id":"n26failure026","type":"text","x":2100,"y":3040,"width":640,"height":300,"text":"# Common Failure Modes\n\nRetrieval miss (right chunk never retrieved) → unrecoverable at generation. Irrelevant context → distracts the model. Contradictory chunks → model must arbitrate. Stale index → answers drift from source-of-truth updates.\n\n**Retriever collapse** (Lewis et al. 2020): on tasks with no clear factual grounding need (e.g. open-ended story generation), the retriever learns to fetch the same document regardless of input — retrieval becomes a no-op."},

		{"id":"n27resources27","type":"text","x":0,"y":3460,"width":1400,"height":320,"text":"# Key Resources\n\n**Original paper:** Lewis et al. 2020, *Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks*.\n\n**Survey:** Gao et al. 2023, *Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Large Language Models: A Survey* — covers naive/advanced/modular RAG.\n\n**IR foundation:** *Introduction to Information Retrieval* (Manning, Raghavan, Schütze), free online.\n\n**Framework:** RAGAS docs — RAG evaluation metrics in practice."},

		{"id":"g7casestudy07","type":"group","x":-40,"y":3900,"width":2820,"height":420,"label":"Layer 7 — Case Study: The Original RAG Paper (Lewis et al. 2020)","color":"6"},

		{"id":"n28dprbart028","type":"text","x":0,"y":3960,"width":640,"height":340,"text":"# Architecture: DPR + BART\n\nRetriever: DPR (Dense Passage Retriever) — a BERT bi-encoder retrieving via Maximum Inner Product Search (MIPS) over a 21M-passage Wikipedia index.\n\nGenerator: BART, a 400M-param seq2seq transformer. Retrieved passages are concatenated with the query as generator input.\n\nDocument encoder + index stay **frozen**; only the query encoder and BART are fine-tuned — end-to-end, with no supervision on which document is 'correct.'"},
		{"id":"n29seqtoken029","type":"text","x":700,"y":3960,"width":640,"height":340,"text":"# RAG-Sequence vs RAG-Token\n\nTwo formulations from the paper, both trained by marginalizing over the top-k retrieved documents:\n\n- **RAG-Sequence** — one retrieved document drives generation of the *entire* output sequence.\n- **RAG-Token** — can draw on a *different* retrieved document for each generated token, letting the model blend content from multiple sources.\n\nSame underlying probabilistic idea, different granularity of 'which document am I trusting right now.'"},
		{"id":"n30results030","type":"text","x":1400,"y":3960,"width":640,"height":340,"text":"# Results & Knowledge Editing\n\nSOTA on open-domain QA at publication: Natural Questions 44.5% EM, WebQuestions 68.0%, TriviaQA 56.8%, CuratedTrec 45.2% — beating both a pure parametric model (T5-11B) and a pure extractive one (DPR alone).\n\n**Knowledge editing without retraining:** swapping the retrieval index (2016 → 2018 Wikipedia dump) correctly updates answers to time-sensitive questions. The paper's core empirical case for non-parametric memory."},
		{"id":"n31halludata031","type":"text","x":2100,"y":3960,"width":640,"height":340,"text":"# Hallucination & Diversity, Quantified\n\nRAG answers 11.8% of Natural Questions correctly even when the retrieved passages *don't* contain the answer (parametric fallback) — vs 0% for purely extractive models.\n\nOn Jeopardy-style question generation, human raters judged RAG more factual than plain BART in 42.7% vs 7.1% of cases.\n\nRAG-Sequence generations were also more diverse: 83.5% distinct trigrams vs BART's 70.7%, with no explicit diversity decoding."}
	],
